~ start
NEWS ANNOUNCER: ...repeating our top story tonight, TANAW (Tropical Atmospheric Navigation and Advanced Weather Bureau) is tracking a severe weather disturbance expected to make landfall this coming November 8. While the storm is still offshore, local government units in Kanlungan City have already proactively declared a suspension of classes for all levels starting tomorrow to allow families time to prepare. 

NEWS ANNOUNCER: ...Residents in low-lying agricultural valleys are highly advised to secure their properties and prepare for heavy rainfall and flash floods. Please stay tuned for further evacuation notices...

DODONG: Sweet! No classes tomorrow! That means I can continue playing my gameboy.

NONI: No school? Yay! Kuya, let's play outside tomorrow in the rain! Kiki can join too!

VALERIE: Be quiet, both of you! Didn't you hear the TV? It's not a regular rainy day. It's a storm!

PILI: Ernesto... did you hear that? The news mentioned the low-lying valleys.
PILI: ...That's us here in Taklub.
PILI: November 8... that’s Friday. That's Ollie's birthday.

ERNESTO: No... 
ERNESTO: Not this week. 
ERNESTO: The news always exaggerates things to scare people in the city, Pili. Our family has lived in Taklub for three generations and not a single drop of floodwater has ever touched this soil. The news anchor doesn't know how strong our coconut trees are. 
ERNESTO: Plus the Millgate trucks are scheduled to pick up the copra by Tuesday morning. If the drying beds get soaked tonight, the moisture will rot the entire batch. 
ERNESTO: Three months of labor will be gone in one single night.

PILI: Ernesto, forget about the harvest for a second. If the storm is that strong, the river at the center of the village might overflow. 
PILI: I... I wanted this Sunday to be perfect for our boy. I wanted to bake his cake. But maybe... maybe we should check our emergency bags just in case? 

ERNESTO: No one is running away anywhere. We survived past storms because these coconut trees shield us.
ERNESTO: This Millgate deal... it’s our only chance to finally breathe this year. 
ERNESTO: I am not letting a little wind take away the one deal that can feed this family for the rest of the year. We are giving our boy a proper birthday right here in his own home. 

PILI: ...You're right, Ernesto. The trees have always kept us safe before. God wouldn't give us this big Millgate deal just to take it away right before our boy's birthday.

KIKI: Nay?

ERNESTO: Dodong, stop playing with that gadget and find the extra blue tarpaulins in the shed. We are tying down the drying roofs right now before the rain starts.

OLLIE: …

Nanay always told us our village is named Taklub because it means to cover. She said our coconut trees are here to keep us safe from everything outside. 

But after that news report, Nanay’s hands began to shake. She kept looking at me so sadly. I’ve never seen her hands do that before. Even Tatay’s smile faded.

I'm sure the trees will protect us like always, right?

I'll still get my birthday cake like they promised. I have to.

=> END